C interpreter optimization measurements, 2026-10-24

Cheaper interrupt polling and a larger decoded instruction cache improve
Gabriel throughput by 25.7% overall (1.2573x), reducing elapsed time by 20.5%.
Polling alone improves throughput by 9.5% (1.0951x). The cache adds a further
14.8% over the polling-only interpreter; these gains combine multiplicatively.
The aggregate is the unweighted geometric mean of 29 workload time ratios.

The combined version has lower median time on all 29 workloads. TAKR improves
2.67x, FRPOLY10R2 1.90x, FRPOLY5R2 1.88x, DDERIV 1.58x, and BROWSE 1.49x.
Small changes on individual workloads should be treated cautiously because
host frequencies and background activity were not controlled exclusively.

Relative to the unchanged Alpha-derived interpreter's earlier three boots,
C's aggregate elapsed-time gap falls from 52.2% to 21.1%. The new C median is
below Alpha for TAK, CTAK, and TAKR. FFT remains about 3.98x slower than Alpha;
these changes do not address its floating-point handling overhead.

Implementation

CPollInterrupt first reads the three request fields with relaxed atomic loads.
When all are clear it returns immediately, avoiding three atomic exchanges
on the normal instruction path. Pending requests still use the original
atomic exchanges, halt priority, and trap-mode checks. Every existing polling
site remains in place, including polling on each guest instruction. The
three fields are checked independently because a producer can publish a
stop or trap payload before the stop_interpreter flag.

The decoded instruction cache grows from 2048 to 65536 entries. The new
VLM_C_ICACHE_ENTRIES CMake setting accepts a power of two of at least 64.
At 40 bytes per entry, the default uses 2.5 MiB rather than 80 KiB. Cache
indexing, decoding, invalidation, and the 64-entry fill block are unchanged.
Floating-point semantics and the existing -fno-fast-math override are unchanged.

Cache sizing

Exploratory sizing used one fresh boot per candidate, seven samples per
workload, compared against the earlier three-boot C baseline. All candidates
include the polling change. cache-sizing.csv preserves this first-boot
comparison separately from the repeated final measurements.

Entries    Cache memory    Speedup over original C
   2048        80 KiB       1.0906x  (poll-1)
  16384       640 KiB       1.2376x
  65536       2.5 MiB       1.2530x
 262144        10 MiB       1.2521x

64K was chosen because it matched 256K overall with one-quarter the memory.
Its additional benefit over 16K was concentrated in large-integer polynomial
workloads. A single sizing boot is not enough to resolve very small differences
between sizes; the selected 64K configuration was then repeated twice more.
The setting remains configurable for other workloads and hosts.

Repeated measurements

Original C, polling-only C, optimized C, and Alpha each use the median of
three boot medians, with seven timed batches per workload per boot. The
original C and Alpha measurements are reused from ../2026-10-24. A new run
of the exact original C executable differed from the earlier baseline by
only 0.21% in aggregate (slower), recorded in baseline-check.csv.

Per-boot aggregate speedups against the fixed original C median were:
  Polling only: 1.0906x, 1.1054x, 1.0897x
  Poll + 64K:   1.2530x, 1.2799x, 1.2492x
These ranges describe observed variation, not statistical confidence intervals.

Times below are milliseconds per canonical suite invocation, not per inner
loop iteration. Speedup is original C time / optimized C time. summary.csv
also records every variant's minimum and maximum boot median.

