Protocol and reproducibility

Same Ryzen 7 PRO 7840U host, GCC 16.2.1, Release, VLM_FAST=ON, VLM_NATIVE=ON,
logical CPU 6, nice 10, powersave governor with boost enabled, and Genera 8.6
world as the baseline experiment. The CPU was not isolated and frequencies
were not locked. No host builds ran during timed measurements. Tests used
one disposable Genera at a time on a private Xvfb display with GLX disabled.

The same compiled suite.vbin and driver.vbin were reused without recompiling.
World, guest code, source, and fixed batch-count hashes were verified unchanged.
The suite's original TIMED-DURATION measures elapsed REAL ticks at 977 Hz;
loading, compilation, warm-up, and file I/O are outside the measured intervals.
Ephemeral GC is enabled and dynamic GC disabled. See ../../README for the
full protocol. These results measure Gabriel workloads, not interactive speed.

Nine additional boots produced 1827 timed batches, each lasting 0.153-1.095 s:
  poll-1, cache16k-1, cache64k-1, cache256k-1, baselinecheck-1,
  poll-2, cache64k-2, poll-3, cache64k-3.
Every boot reached the completion marker and exited with status 0. All saved
warm-up return values match the original C and Alpha results byte-for-byte.

The baseline binary was preserved before editing. All optimized binaries
were built from the same changes, with only VLM_C_ICACHE_ENTRIES varied.
The final build/src/genera is byte-identical to the measured 64K executable.
metadata.json records hashes, settings, run order, and derived aggregates.

Recompute the final comparison from the repository root:

  python3 benchmarks/gabriel/compare.py \
    --group 'baseline=benchmarks/gabriel/results/2026-10-24/c-[123].csv' \
    --group 'poll=benchmarks/gabriel/results/2026-10-24-optimization/poll-[123].csv' \
    --group 'optimized=benchmarks/gabriel/results/2026-10-24-optimization/cache64k-[123].csv' \
    --group 'alpha=benchmarks/gabriel/results/2026-10-24/alpha-[123].csv' \
    --output benchmarks/gabriel/results/2026-10-24-optimization/summary.csv

For new measurements, build the polling-only variant with
-DVLM_C_ICACHE_ENTRIES=2048 and the combined variant with 65536. Use run.py
with seven samples, the same counts.json, the same compiled guest code, and
new run labels. The original baseline used commit a183dd8 before these edits.

Validation

The final CTest suite passed all 1536 CPU checks, including new checks for
independently published interrupt requests, all four trap modes, halt
priority, cache collisions, packed odd-PC boundary crossings, and explicit
invalidation after guest code modification. The cache-size checks passed
at 16K, 64K, and 256K entries. CMake rejects too-small, non-power-of-two,
and nonnumeric sizes (tested with 32, 96, and "no").

The interactive regression logged in as lisp-machine, forced the BAR/GC
regression in tests/bar-gc.lisp, opened Genera Concepts, Genera User's Guide,
and Genera Workbook, and ran Help Commands :Format Full. The GC assertion
passed and all documents and command output displayed without a Lisp trap.
A separate Halt Genera check completed its FEP confirmation and exited with
status 0, without the former stack-overflow diagnostic.

The first document-test helper reached the SIGTERM exit confirmation but
exceeded its three-second cleanup timeout and was killed by the helper. The
full document/GC test was repeated with a 15-second cleanup timeout and exited
with status 0. This cleanup observation is separate from the passing guest
checks and the clean Halt Genera test; both attempts' host logs are retained.
